   IS THE CONSTANT PART OF THE SAME PATH? The cdipol ramp is ii*cutoff, and the taper breaks its antisymmetry, so mean(cvdip) can scale with the dipole and the constant would then belong to the dipole-feedback
   path too. Otherwise it is a separate G=0 bookkeeping term. Measured: mean of the feedback change -0.00000 eV against the total constant -0.08195 eV, i.e. 0.0% of it. Near 100% means one open item, not two.

   THE DRIVER of that feedback is the solvent charge's DIPOLE, so the question is whether P_off* improves the profile while degrading the dipole:
                                           case  dipole (e A)      error
                                  DFT reference      16.70259   +0.00000
                                       baseline      16.40810   -0.29449
                                   exact P_off*      16.65316   -0.04943

   READING, and note which way it comes out. The natural reading would be that P_off* drives the ramp with a WORSE dipole. If instead the dipole moves TOWARD the reference while the potential still
   degrades, that reading is refuted and the conclusion is stronger: at a charge profile AND a dipole both closer to the reference, cvhar_z + cvdip + l0_inv(charge) moves FURTHER from the DFT total
   potential -- so neither input is the problem and the construction of the potential itself is, which means the cdipol taper form, cvhar_z, and the G=0 handling. Measured dipole error -0.29449 -> -0.04943 e A (CLOSER, 5.96x).
   No mechanism is offered for WHY, deliberately. In particular the story that the baseline's wrong dipole was compensating an error in the ramp form would fit, and it is the same shape as the
   solute-side compensation story that just failed its own gate, so it needs its own gate before anyone states it. L1 and the dipole are different functionals of the same profile and nothing forces them
   to move together -- which is why the aggregate has to include the potential, as the stop rule says.

  MIXED IS NOT A PASS. The stop rule names aggregate charge, the POTENTIAL, cross energy and the full self-energy including the bound-ion mutual term. If the potential or the mutual term goes the
  wrong way while the energy gap closes, that is the signature of compensating errors -- which is the thing this line of work exists to remove -- and the energy closure cannot stand as the verdict on its own,
  any more than the ionic channel's 10.6-fold improvement could when the aggregate moved 0.03%.

  If the aggregate does not improve here, STOP: the compensation is not the binding problem and the next place to look is the other inputs and the self-consistent coupling, not training. If it does improve,
  step 3 asks whether the existing head can produce delta_p* within its own basis and its c_max = 0.25 clipping.

